  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">LINKING COMMUNITY-ENGAGED RESEARCH WITH REFORESTATION TO ACHIEVE LASTING CONSERVATION IN THE ECUADORIAN CHOC</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THIS PROJECT, ENTITLED LINKING COMMUNITY-ENGAGED RESEARCH WITH REFORESTATION TO ACHIEVE LASTING CONSERVATION IN THE ECUADORIAN CHOC PART II. SOCIAL DYNAMICS FITS UNDER THE CATEGORY OF THEME 1 SUSTAINABLE RESOURCE USE. THE OVER-ARCHING GOAL IS TO DEVELOP A MODEL FOR HABITAT RESTORATION FOR PRIORITY HABITATS IN LATIN AMERICA THAT INTEGRATES TWO KEY INNOVATIONS EXPERIMENTAL TESTS OF REFORESTATION METHODS AND GRASSROOTS INVOLVEMENT AND BUY-IN BY LOCAL COMMUNITIES. IN THE FIRST PHASE, WHICH IS ALREADY UP AND RUNNING, WE ARE USING A LANDSCAPE-LEVEL REFORESTATION EXPERIMENT TO RIGOROUSLY TEST THE EFFICACY OF COMPETING RESTORATION METHODOLOGIES FOR BIODIVERSITY CONSERVATION AND ECONOMIC IMPACT. IN THE SECOND PHASE, WHICH WE ARE REQUESTING SUPPORT FOR IN THE CURRENT PROPOSAL, WE TAKE A BOTTOM UP GRASSROOTS SOCIOLOGICAL APPROACH TO UNDERSTAND THE ECONOMIC AND SOCIAL FACTORS THAT SHAPE LOCAL RESIDENTS WILLINGNESS TO ENGAGE IN HABITAT RESTORATION. IN PHASE THREE, SLATED TO BEGIN IN LATE 2024, WE COMBINE OUR UNDERSTANDING OF WHAT WORKS FROM BOTH AN ECOLOGICAL PERSPECTIVE VIA THE LANDSCAPE LEVEL EXPERIMENT AND A SOCIO-ECONOMIC PERSPECTIVE VIA SOCIOLOGICAL DATA COLLECTION AND INTEGRATION AT HOUSEHOLD, COMMUNITY, AND INSTITUTIONAL LEVELS.</narrative>
  </description>
